# Golden-file tree pretty-prints — written and compared byte-exact by
# `tests/analysis/syntactic/test_corpus.cpp::checkGoldenTree`. Force LF
# regardless of `core.autocrlf` so a Windows checkout doesn't introduce
# CRLF and break the byte-equality compare on first run.
*.tree text eol=lf

# Test-corpus source inputs parsed end-to-end by `test_corpus.cpp`. Forcing
# LF prevents `core.autocrlf` from injecting CR bytes the shipped schemas
# need to declare explicitly as `Whitespace` lexemes (see e.g. c-subset.lang.json).
tests/corpus/** text eol=lf

# Shipped language + target config JSONs. Pinned to LF for the same reason
# as `tests/corpus/**`: the lexer treats `\r` as an explicit Whitespace lexeme,
# and the `D5_5_LiftOptOutRespected` test (and similar) substring-match against
# the file with bare `\n` separators. Without this pin, a Windows `core.autocrlf`
# checkout rewrites configs to CRLF and the test fails on CI even though local
# tests pass.
src/dss-config/** text eol=lf

# Curated runnable-corpus sources (examples/<lang>/<name>/). The V2-4 Part C
# expect-error harness asserts diagnostic POSITIONS (line:col) resolved from
# these source bytes, so pin LF: a Windows `core.autocrlf` checkout that
# injected CR would shift byte offsets between the compiler's read and the
# runner's source read, mis-resolving the asserted line:col.
examples/** text eol=lf

# FC17.9(h) C23 `#embed` resource files. A committed binary resource (e.g. the
# c23_embed example's answer.bin, whose bytes include 0x0D CR / 0x0A LF / 0x1A
# SUB) MUST NOT be CRLF-normalized by the `examples/** text eol=lf` rule above —
# that would corrupt the bytes `#embed` reads, failing ONLY on fresh clones / CI.
# This later line WINS by last-match precedence, marking every examples `.bin`
# strictly binary (byte-identical on every checkout). The self-witnessing example
# (blob[1] == 13) is the red canary if this is ever lost.
examples/**/*.bin binary

# `.dsshir` HIR text-format goldens — emitted with LF by `emitHir` and compared
# byte-exact by the HIR text + lowering golden tests (test_hir_text.cpp,
# test_hir_lowering_*.cpp). Without this a Windows `core.autocrlf` checkout
# rewrites them to CRLF and the byte-compare diverges on `\r` (CI: windows-msvc).
*.dsshir text eol=lf

# LSP replay session goldens — line-compared by test_lsp_replay.cpp. The reader
# already strips trailing CR, but pin LF so the goldens are platform-stable.
tests/lsp/sessions/** text eol=lf

# `.probes` reference-conformance corpora — one probe per line, read line-wise by
# the differential accept/reject oracle (tests/conformance/). Pinned because the
# extension arrived WITHOUT a pin and immediately landed CRLF on disk: with
# core.autocrlf=false and no rule here, `git add` does NOT normalise, so the CR
# bytes would have entered the commit. ★ AND A CRLF PROBE FILE FAILS IN THE WORST
# WAY — not loudly. A reader that strips `\n` and keeps `\r` compares `token\r`
# against `token`, so EVERY probe silently mismatches and the oracle reports a
# clean run over a corpus it never parsed. That exact `ran\r` defect has already
# been measured in this repo, which is why this is a pin and not a convention.
*.probes text eol=lf

# THE SCRIPTS IN scripts/ — including the twin `.sh`/`.ps1` guard pairs ctest runs
# as `anchor_registry_guard`, `line_endings_guard` and `orphan_tests_guard`.
# Pinned for the same reason `real-examples/**` is: these run on Windows, WSL,
# macOS and arm64, and a CRLF `.sh` fails on a POSIX leg at the shebang with a
# useless error. ★ THE SHARPER REASON IS SELF-REFERENCE: `check-line-endings.sh`
# is itself one of these files, so an unpinned CRLF rewrite can break the very
# guard that would have reported it — a guard that cannot run reads exactly like
# a guard that found nothing. Left unpinned until now, so both orphan-guard
# twins were LF by an author's care rather than by mechanism, which is the
# distinction this repo's own wiring comment draws: "an invariant nothing
# executes is a convention."
# ⓘ 2026-08-19: these three globs said `tools/**` until the whole directory
# was merged into `scripts/` (one directory per script, siblings inside).
# Retargeting them WIDENED the pin: cmake-import, corpus-census,
# pragma-profile-census and local-build already lived under scripts/ and had
# never been pinned at all — they were LF by an author's care, which is the
# exact distinction this comment draws against.
scripts/**/*.sh  text eol=lf
scripts/**/*.ps1 text eol=lf
scripts/**/*.py  text eol=lf

# The real-example HARNESS DRIVERS (real-examples/c/sqlite/). Their whole promise
# is "clone the repo and run ONE command", on ANY host — so a fresh clone must
# not be able to break them before they start. `core.autocrlf=true` is the
# Git-for-Windows INSTALLER DEFAULT, and under it a Windows clone rewrites
# `build-and-test.sh` / `check-source-coherence.sh` to CRLF; bash then fails on
# the stray `\r` (classically `$'\r': command not found`) and the driver is dead
# on arrival on the very host it was meant to run from. MEASURED here only as
# the NEGATIVE: this workstation has `core.autocrlf=false` (both --local and
# --global), which is exactly why the hazard has never been observed in-tree —
# it is latent on a DIFFERENT machine, i.e. the same shape as the host-locking
# defect D-HARNESS-CROSS-HOST-ANY-TARGET exists to end.
# Listed by EXTENSION rather than as `real-examples/** text eol=lf`, because the
# broad form would also force text normalization onto any binary fixture added
# later — the corruption the `examples/**/*.bin binary` rule above had to be
# added to undo.
real-examples/**/*.sh   text eol=lf
real-examples/**/*.ps1  text eol=lf
real-examples/**/*.py   text eol=lf
real-examples/**/*.json text eol=lf

# ── D-REPO-GITATTRIBUTES-PINS-EOL-FOR-CONFIGS-BUT-NOT-FOR-SOURCES ──────────
# THE SOURCE TREE ITSELF. Every rule above pins a CONFIG, a GOLDEN or a
# DRIVER — i.e. a file some test reads byte-exactly — and not one of them
# covers a `.cpp`. MEASURED 2026-08-06 before this block existed:
# `git check-attr text eol -- src/link/linker.cpp tests/link/test_pe_writer.cpp`
# reported `unspecified` for both, so NO C/C++/CMake source anywhere in this
# tree had a pinned line ending.
#
# That is not a hypothetical: the defect had ALREADY LANDED SIX TIMES.
# `git grep -I -l -P '\r$' HEAD -- src tests real-examples scripts` returned
# src/CMakeLists.txt, src/core/compiler.{cpp,hpp}, src/core/export.hpp,
# src/main.cpp and tests/ffi/test_shipped_lib_descriptor.cpp — the last
# WHOLLY CRLF (6206 CRs). They are normalised in their own dedicated commit;
# a pin does not retroactively rewrite committed content.
#
# HOW IT GETS IN: a tool's platform default, never a decision. The measured
# instance was a delegated agent editing fixtures through Python
# `pathlib.write_text`, whose `newline` default applies platform translation
# and so silently rewrites LF -> CRLF on Windows. This pin takes that choice
# away from the tool: the clean filter normalises on `git add` regardless of
# `core.autocrlf`, so the bytes this repo STORES can no longer depend on
# which OS an author (or an agent) happened to run on.
#
# ⚠ These globs are LAST, and last-match wins — so each one is checked
# against the `examples/**/*.bin binary` rule above: none of `*.c`, `*.h`,
# `*.cpp`, `*.hpp`, `*.cmake`, `CMakeLists.txt`, `*.md`, `/VERSION` can match
# a `.bin`, so the `#embed` resource bytes that rule exists to protect stay
# binary. VERIFIED, not assumed: `git ls-files --eol` over exactly this glob
# set reports 1346 `i/lf` + 9 `i/crlf` + 1 `i/none` and ZERO `-text`, i.e.
# git detects no binary content anywhere in the captured set.
*.c            text eol=lf
*.h            text eol=lf
*.cpp          text eol=lf
*.hpp          text eol=lf
CMakeLists.txt text eol=lf
*.cmake        text eol=lf

# `*.md` and `/VERSION` ride the same block because the SAME normalisation
# commit rewrites them: the repo-wide sweep (`git grep -I -l -P '\r$' HEAD`,
# no pathspec) found four MORE CRLF blobs outside src/tests — the three
# `.plans/*.md` files and `VERSION`. Normalising a file whose extension is
# NOT pinned would be the same defect scheduled to recur, so they are pinned
# in the same breath they are fixed. `VERSION` is anchored to the repo root
# (`/VERSION`) because it is a single specific file, not an extension class.
*.md           text eol=lf
/VERSION       text eol=lf

# Binary TEST FIXTURES (`tests/**/data/`). Committed object files, shared libraries and
# other byte-exact inputs a test parses. Declared `binary` for the same reason as the
# `examples/**/*.bin` rule above, and the reason is worth restating: WITHOUT a declaration
# these ride on git's binary AUTO-DETECTION, which happened to classify them correctly —
# i.e. they worked by inference rather than by declaration. MEASURED 2026-08-06 (TF-C124):
# `tests/ffi/data/libdssver.so.1`, a real GNU ld 2.42 output whose versym/verdef bytes a
# reader test decodes, was covered by nothing in this file. The EOL pin added the same day
# named SOURCE extensions and forgot that test DATA is the category most likely to need the
# OPPOSITE treatment — a pin's blast radius is exactly what it names, and what it omits is
# invisible (D-REPO-GITATTRIBUTES-DOES-NOT-COVER-BINARY-TEST-FIXTURES).
tests/**/data/** binary

# Binary EXAMPLE fixtures: prebuilt shared libraries a runnable example loads.
# `examples/c-subset/environ_alias_object_identity/` ships two gcc-built `.so`s
# (x86_64 + aarch64) because an OBJECT-IDENTITY property cannot be witnessed by
# ONE image — the example needs a second image DSS did not build, and shipping
# it prebuilt means the corpus needs no host cross-compiler at test time
# (D-LK-ELF-COPY-RELOC-CLAIMS-ONE-NAME-OF-AN-ALIAS-SET).
# ★ WITHOUT THIS LINE THE BYTES ARE CORRUPTED, not merely unpinned: the
# `examples/** text eol=lf` rule near the top of this file applies the text
# clean/smudge filter to EVERY path under examples/, so an ELF whose bytes
# happen to contain 0x0D would be rewritten on checkout and the `.so` would
# fail to load — the same defect the `examples/**/*.bin binary` rule exists to
# prevent, and the same category the note above says is the one most likely to
# be forgotten. Placed LAST so last-match precedence beats every text rule.
examples/**/*.so binary

# The shipped RUNTIME's generated object cache (D-RUNTIME-DSS-SHIPS-NO-IMPLEMENTATION-HALF).
# `src/dss-config/runtime/platform/dist/` holds COMPILED OUTPUT — object bytes for a
# target, keyed by content — inside a directory tree that the `src/dss-config/** text
# eol=lf` rule near the top of this file otherwise claims wholesale.
#
# ★ THIS IS NOT PRE-EMPTIVE TIDYING; THE TWO DECLARATIONS ACTIVELY DISAGREE TODAY.
# ✔MEASURED 2026-08-17 on a probe object written into that tree:
#     git check-ignore -v  -> .gitignore:87 (ignored, as intended)
#     git check-attr       -> text: set, eol: lf      ← on a FILE OF OBJECT BYTES
# So `.gitignore` says "generated, never tracked" while `.gitattributes` says "text,
# normalise every CR". The contradiction is inert only for as long as nothing makes one
# of those files tracked, and the two ways that happens are both ordinary: a `git add -f`
# while debugging a cache miss, or the shipped `dist/release/` tree becoming COMMITTED so
# a package can carry it — which is exactly what the operator ruling wants that tree for.
# On the day either happens, checkout rewrites 0x0D bytes inside a relocatable object and
# the corruption is silent: the file is present, the size is close, and the link fails (or
# worse, does not) far from the cause.
#
# ⚠ THIS IS THE THIRD INSTANCE OF ONE DEFECT, so it is a pattern and not an accident —
# `tests/**/data/**` and `examples/**/*.so` above are the first two, and both were written
# only AFTER a broad `text eol=lf` rule had already claimed binary bytes it did not know
# about. The lesson each time: a directory-wide text pin's blast radius is exactly what it
# NAMES, and what it omits is invisible. Declared here in the same change that creates the
# tree, rather than after the third corruption.
#
# Placed after `examples/**/*.so` so last-match precedence beats `src/dss-config/**`.
src/dss-config/runtime/platform/dist/** binary
